// Foundation's `@Derive(Tagged)`: an enum's variants as numbers, and back.
//
//   @Derive(Tagged)
//   enum Material {
//       Flat
//       Frosted
//   }
//
// generates
//
//   function code_Material(v: borrow Material) -> Int
//   function Material_fromCode(id: Int) -> Material
//
// The generated names are glued to the enum's exact name — `code_Material`,
// `Material_fromCode` — and that is a fixed contract other tooling reads, the
// the same source-glued naming rule as the other Foundation derives.
//
// # Why this exists
//
// Kira enums have no ordinal. A program needing a variant as a number — to fold
// it into a hash, to write it into a vertex attribute a shader reads, to send it
// across a seam — writes the table by hand:
//
//   function materialCode(m: Material) -> Int {
//       match m { Flat -> return 1; Frosted -> return 2; }
//   }
//
// and then owns two sources of truth that no compiler checks against each other.
// Inserting a variant renumbers one and not the other, silently. Generating the
// table from the declaration makes that impossible: there is one list, and it is
// the enum.
//
// # The numbers are declaration order
//
// A variant's code is its position, counting from zero. That is stable as long
// as the enum is only appended to — which is the same discipline every wire
// format here already keeps, and the reason a reorder is a breaking change
// rather than a tidy-up.
//
// A payload-carrying variant gets a code like any other; the payload is not
// part of it. `Material_fromCode` answers the *first* variant for a number
// naming none, because a total function is what a decoder needs and there is no
// variant to spell "unknown" with unless the enum declares one.

comptime macro Tagged {
    kind { derive }
    appliesTo { enum }
    expand(target: Declaration) -> Syntax {
        var toCode: [Syntax] = []
        var fromCode: [Syntax] = []
        var index: Int = 0
        var first: Syntax = quote { }
        for variant in target.fields {
            // A variant is reflected as a field whose name is the variant's.
            // Matching it needs no payload binding: the code is the position,
            // and a payload does not change it.
            toCode.append(quote {
                #{variant.name} -> return #{index}
            })
            fromCode.append(quote {
                if id == #{index} {
                    return .#{variant.name}
                }
            })
            if index == 0 {
                first = quote { .#{variant.name} }
            }
            index = index + 1
        }
        return quote {
            function code_#{target.name}(value: borrow #{target.name}) -> Int {
                match value {
                    #{toCode}
                }
            }

            function #{target.name}_fromCode(id: Int) -> #{target.name} {
                #{fromCode}
                return #{first}
            }
        }
    }
}
